1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,900
Hey, in this video, we're gonna talk about the difference

2
00:00:01,900 --> 00:00:04,020
between chat models and reasoning models.

3
00:00:04,360 --> 00:00:06,280
We'll learn about why you should use different types

4
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:07,780
of models depending upon the task.

5
00:00:08,020 --> 00:00:09,380
So firstly, chat models.

6
00:00:09,660 --> 00:00:11,820
If you've ever used something like ChatGPT,

7
00:00:12,340 --> 00:00:13,380
then you've used a chat model.

8
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:15,740
A chat model is basically a large language model

9
00:00:15,740 --> 00:00:18,440
that uses a sequence of messages in a message history

10
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:21,280
as inputs and returns messages as outputs.

11
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:23,140
On the left here, you can see we've got

12
00:00:23,140 --> 00:00:26,080
what is digital marketing and that is a user message.

13
00:00:26,660 --> 00:00:29,640
You can also see that we've got a response from OpenAI.

14
00:00:30,080 --> 00:00:31,480
That is an AI message.

15
00:00:31,960 --> 00:00:33,280
There are three different types of messages

16
00:00:33,280 --> 00:00:34,940
that generally chat models expose.

17
00:00:35,240 --> 00:00:36,700
There's the developer system message.

18
00:00:37,240 --> 00:00:38,300
There's the user messages,

19
00:00:38,440 --> 00:00:40,380
which are the messages that you would generally type.

20
00:00:40,740 --> 00:00:42,020
And then there's the AI messages

21
00:00:42,020 --> 00:00:43,760
that come back from a chat model.

22
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:45,760
There are lots of large language model providers

23
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:46,480
to choose from.

24
00:00:46,760 --> 00:00:49,700
For example, we have OpenAI with their GPT-X series.

25
00:00:49,940 --> 00:00:51,060
We also have Anthropic,

26
00:00:51,160 --> 00:00:53,540
which offers Claude Sonnet and Claude Opus models.

27
00:00:53,540 --> 00:00:56,660
There is also Meta with their free open source models,

28
00:00:56,780 --> 00:00:57,980
which are the Llama models.

29
00:00:58,520 --> 00:01:00,060
There is also Google Gemini.

30
00:01:00,380 --> 00:01:02,060
They have both a Google Gemini Pro

31
00:01:02,060 --> 00:01:04,380
and a Google Gemini Flash series of models.

32
00:01:04,660 --> 00:01:06,180
There's Mistral and many more.

33
00:01:06,360 --> 00:01:07,740
Some of the characteristics that you'll find

34
00:01:07,740 --> 00:01:09,940
with chat models is that they are actually very quick.

35
00:01:10,260 --> 00:01:11,320
They have low latency.

36
00:01:11,960 --> 00:01:13,140
These models respond quickly

37
00:01:13,140 --> 00:01:15,920
and often stream the tokens to you word by word.

38
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:18,640
They're very good in terms of the reason capabilities.

39
00:01:19,120 --> 00:01:21,720
For example, Claude or GPT-4O are able to solve

40
00:01:21,720 --> 00:01:23,680
easy to medium difficulty tasks.

41
00:01:23,980 --> 00:01:25,960
Reasoning models are a newer type of model

42
00:01:25,960 --> 00:01:28,460
that uses something called test time compute scaling.

43
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:31,440
And basically, you can think of test time compute scaling

44
00:01:31,440 --> 00:01:33,500
as basically allocating more time

45
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:34,880
to thinking and reasoning

46
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:37,440
before returning the result or the response.

47
00:01:38,020 --> 00:01:41,340
So in general, these models take a lot longer

48
00:01:41,340 --> 00:01:43,780
because they're actually allocating time at execution

49
00:01:43,780 --> 00:01:45,380
to think through the problem rationally

50
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:48,020
and then coming up with an overall solution.

51
00:01:48,720 --> 00:01:49,540
Let's have a look at this

52
00:01:49,540 --> 00:01:51,480
so we can see that it's got multiple turns

53
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:54,240
and it has a bit of reasoning.

54
00:01:54,620 --> 00:01:56,660
That output then goes into the second turn

55
00:01:56,660 --> 00:01:58,100
which then has some input

56
00:01:58,100 --> 00:01:59,780
and then that goes into the reasoning

57
00:01:59,780 --> 00:02:01,440
which then produces an output.

58
00:02:01,540 --> 00:02:04,040
So you can see it's a multi-turn experience

59
00:02:04,040 --> 00:02:07,020
and basically that means that it's got more thinking time.

60
00:02:07,240 --> 00:02:08,320
So when you ask it a question,

61
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:09,940
we'll allocate more time

62
00:02:09,940 --> 00:02:13,120
before actually responding to you or your query.

63
00:02:13,240 --> 00:02:14,820
So the kind of characteristics you'll get

64
00:02:14,820 --> 00:02:17,800
when you're using a reasoning model is increased latency.

65
00:02:18,280 --> 00:02:20,580
Now, OpenAI, which have released GPT-5

66
00:02:20,580 --> 00:02:22,680
have two modes for using this.

67
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:25,800
There is the fast mode and there is the thinking mode.

68
00:02:26,120 --> 00:02:27,920
You'll find that if you use the thinking mode,

69
00:02:27,920 --> 00:02:29,840
it has additional latency.

70
00:02:30,120 --> 00:02:31,420
It takes longer to come back

71
00:02:31,420 --> 00:02:33,340
and longer for you to get that response.

72
00:02:33,760 --> 00:02:36,400
Now, due to this test time compute reasoning,

73
00:02:37,120 --> 00:02:39,980
these models will definitely outperform chat models.

74
00:02:40,240 --> 00:02:42,780
If you have a very hard problem

75
00:02:42,780 --> 00:02:43,840
or you're not sure

76
00:02:44,300 --> 00:02:46,040
or you don't mind suffering

77
00:02:46,040 --> 00:02:48,560
with some additional time for the response to come back,

78
00:02:48,820 --> 00:02:49,940
if you want to make sure

79
00:02:49,940 --> 00:02:51,540
and it's a very important piece of work,

80
00:02:52,040 --> 00:02:53,560
definitely use a reasoning model.

81
00:02:53,820 --> 00:02:56,500
They have more time that they spend on the problem

82
00:02:56,500 --> 00:02:58,540
and they will give you more accurate results.

83
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:00,860
Realize that there's always a trade-off here

84
00:03:00,860 --> 00:03:04,000
and you are spending longer waiting for the AI model

85
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:04,880
to get back to you.

86
00:03:05,020 --> 00:03:06,960
Here are some examples of when you should use

87
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:08,540
a chat model versus a reasoning model.

88
00:03:08,760 --> 00:03:10,380
So for example, when we're doing content,

89
00:03:10,560 --> 00:03:11,500
quick emails and posts,

90
00:03:11,960 --> 00:03:13,540
but when we might use a reasoning model

91
00:03:13,540 --> 00:03:16,020
is technical documentation or research analysis.

92
00:03:16,640 --> 00:03:18,120
For example, if we look at problem solving,

93
00:03:18,120 --> 00:03:20,240
basic calculations or simple Q&A,

94
00:03:20,480 --> 00:03:21,860
but if we were using a reasoning model,

95
00:03:22,040 --> 00:03:23,800
maybe we'd use that for multi-step debugging.

96
00:03:24,120 --> 00:03:25,260
Okay, so you now know the difference

97
00:03:25,260 --> 00:03:27,020
between a chat model and a reasoning model.

98
00:03:27,280 --> 00:03:28,540
Let's have a look at how we can do this

99
00:03:28,540 --> 00:03:29,300
inside of OpenAI.

100
00:03:29,660 --> 00:03:30,940
Go to chatgbt.com

101
00:03:30,940 --> 00:03:33,360
and then if you go and click in the top left,

102
00:03:33,520 --> 00:03:36,900
you can see that we have the option to use GPT-5.

103
00:03:37,240 --> 00:03:39,360
We've either auto fast thinking

104
00:03:39,360 --> 00:03:41,000
or if you even upgrade even further,

105
00:03:41,380 --> 00:03:42,540
you can get a pro,

106
00:03:42,720 --> 00:03:44,060
which is research grade intelligence.

107
00:03:44,760 --> 00:03:47,660
You can see that auto will basically do model routing

108
00:03:47,660 --> 00:03:49,380
where look at your query and decide,

109
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:52,480
is it gonna be using a fast inference model,

110
00:03:52,620 --> 00:03:53,260
so a chat model,

111
00:03:53,460 --> 00:03:55,360
and also we have the option to do thinking.

112
00:03:55,660 --> 00:03:56,520
So if I say thinking,

113
00:03:56,780 --> 00:04:00,220
and then what we can type is what is data science?

114
00:04:01,420 --> 00:04:02,540
And then what you'll see is

115
00:04:02,540 --> 00:04:04,320
when we then submit our query,

116
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:06,640
you can see that chatgbt is thinking.

117
00:04:08,260 --> 00:04:09,820
If you click on the thinking,

118
00:04:09,840 --> 00:04:11,740
you'll see that you can actually see

119
00:04:11,740 --> 00:04:14,320
the thoughts and the reasoning tokens

120
00:04:14,320 --> 00:04:16,080
that chatgbt decided to do.

121
00:04:16,080 --> 00:04:17,600
Then we get our response.

122
00:04:17,960 --> 00:04:19,980
So we had to wait seven seconds

123
00:04:19,980 --> 00:04:21,840
for the thinking model

124
00:04:21,840 --> 00:04:24,360
to come back with a response for us.

125
00:04:24,780 --> 00:04:26,680
If we contrast this with fast

126
00:04:26,680 --> 00:04:28,480
and I go and load a fast model,

127
00:04:28,860 --> 00:04:30,500
and then I'm gonna keep in the same conversation

128
00:04:30,500 --> 00:04:32,820
and say what is data science?

129
00:04:35,260 --> 00:04:37,980
You'll now see that we don't have any thinking tokens.

130
00:04:38,140 --> 00:04:40,040
We basically get an immediate response.

131
00:04:40,460 --> 00:04:42,980
However, the model isn't thinking about

132
00:04:42,980 --> 00:04:45,420
how best to service our query.

133
00:04:45,740 --> 00:04:47,080
So that is how you can switch.

134
00:04:47,080 --> 00:04:48,280
Now, obviously, if you want,

135
00:04:48,380 --> 00:04:49,920
you can stick on auto mode,

136
00:04:49,980 --> 00:04:53,620
which will look at how complicated your query is,

137
00:04:53,620 --> 00:04:55,960
and it will decide whether to use a fast model

138
00:04:55,960 --> 00:04:58,460
or whether to use a thinking-based reasoning model.

139
00:04:58,620 --> 00:04:59,320
For best practices,

140
00:04:59,940 --> 00:05:02,540
I would recommend for tasks that require increased accuracy,

141
00:05:02,680 --> 00:05:04,720
or you must make sure that the error is less,

142
00:05:05,220 --> 00:05:06,220
use reasoning models.

143
00:05:06,700 --> 00:05:09,220
For tasks such as content generation or copywriting,

144
00:05:09,460 --> 00:05:10,980
you can use a smaller chat model.

145
00:05:11,620 --> 00:05:13,900
Reasoning models also take time to respond.

146
00:05:14,220 --> 00:05:16,020
So you can also have a reasoning model

147
00:05:16,020 --> 00:05:17,960
working on a task for a couple of minutes

148
00:05:17,960 --> 00:05:22,160
whilst you're also using Claude or GPT-40 alongside that.

149
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:23,780
So don't wait two to three minutes

150
00:05:23,780 --> 00:05:24,820
for the task to complete.

151
00:05:25,260 --> 00:05:26,420
Go and work on another task

152
00:05:26,420 --> 00:05:27,940
whilst you're waiting for the reasoning model

153
00:05:27,940 --> 00:05:29,020
to get back to you.

154
00:05:29,180 --> 00:05:30,220
In the next section of the course,

155
00:05:30,220 --> 00:05:32,060
we'll look at how you can use standard practices

156
00:05:32,060 --> 00:05:34,700
such as list generation, sentiment analysis,

157
00:05:35,040 --> 00:05:36,900
and writing clean and clear instruction

158
00:05:36,900 --> 00:05:38,860
to make sure that you get the most out of these models.
